    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is structurally clean and idiomatic for this codebase:\n\n- **Right unit.** `CountHousehold` mirrors the existing `CountNamesakes` almost exactly \u2014 a single-method function that reads the `@Val Owner`, counts against `ownerRepository.findAll()`, and stores the result back on the owner. A maintainer who knows `CountNamesakes` will find `CountHousehold` immediately familiar.\n\n- **Correct placement in the pipeline.** It's wired directly after `AssignHousehold` (which assigns and back-fills `householdId`) and before `countNamesakes`, which is exactly the dependency the javadoc documents. Counting by `householdId` equality \u2014 rather than re-deriving the match via `Households.matches` \u2014 is the natural choice here precisely *because* `AssignHousehold` has already normalized every member onto one id; it avoids duplicating the household-definition logic.\n\n- **Consistent with the established \"stored count drives derived tier\" pattern.** `householdSize` as a persisted `@Column` driving `getMembershipTier()` follows the existing `namesakeCount` precedent, rather than inventing a new mechanism. The `getMembershipTier()` edit is a clean prepend of the GOLD check ahead of the existing SILVER/BRONZE logic, and the \"Derived from the owner's own fields\" framing is preserved.\n\n- **Complete and additive.** The column is added across all four schema dialects, the YAML wiring, the model, and the OpenAPI description, with no changes to unrelated units and no catch-all accretion.\n\n- **No new duplication.** The `findAll()`-filter-count loop is an already-established idiom (`AssignHousehold`, `CountNamesakes`); folding the count into `AssignHousehold` would have overloaded that class with two responsibilities, so keeping it a separate step is the better fit.",
